# config/standalone-commands.list — the loadables that stand in for an
# external command (coreutils, util-linux, procps-ng, findutils, grep, sed,
# gawk, diffutils, tar, gzip, iproute2, ...). One NAME per line; '#' comments
# and blank lines are ignored; every NAME must be in config/bash-loadables.list
# or config/bash-loadables-optional.list (build.sh checks).
#
# build.sh writes the selected ones into builtins/builtins.c as
# bashos_standalone_builtins[], and patches/shim-command-name.patch makes
# such a builtin report errors as the program does: "rm: cannot remove ...",
# not "SCRIPT: line N: rm: ..." or "bash: rm: ...", in scripts, bash -c,
# sh -c and interactive shells. Bash's own builtins (cd, read, kill, printf,
# test, ...) and the loadables not listed here keep bash's form, with the
# script name and line number a script author needs.
#
# The rule (2026-11-23): a loadable is listed when Debian 13 ships an
# executable of that name (as found on the reference host in /usr/bin,
# /usr/sbin, /bin or /sbin) or a standard Debian package does (netstat, pax,
# uuencode, uudecode, at, batch, cal, write, sv, doas, dialog, wg, keyctl),
# and the loadable takes that command's arguments. Loadables named after a
# command but driven by bash-os verbs of their own (ssh connect, sshd, cron
# run, git plumbing, script record, login, passwd) are not listed.

